303
00:22:34,910 --> 00:22:39,569
Scott: And then episode two- He's like, "I've waited 4,000 years for someone to want to read these.

304
00:22:39,990 --> 00:22:43,339
Scott: My whole job has been taking care of these records that no one ever looks

305
00:22:43,480 --> 00:22:43,520
Scott: at.".

306
00:22:43,660 --> 00:22:43,801
Scott: Yep.

307
00:22:43,922 --> 00:22:46,019
Rym: You're the first person to ever want to read this book.

308
00:22:46,500 --> 00:22:48,153
Scott: "How long I've waited for this

309
00:22:48,193 --> 00:22:48,455
Scott: day.".

310
00:22:49,200 --> 00:22:56,200
Rym: And basically, the rest of the anime is a flashback to what is in that book that she's reading.

311
00:22:57,121 --> 00:22:58,800
Scott: Yeah, the tablets are scrolls.

312
00:23:00,681 --> 00:23:04,600
Rym: But what is it about is also not what I expected.

313
00:23:05,021 --> 00:23:12,900
Scott: So it's an alternate history story about basically feudal Japan.

314
00:23:14,361 --> 00:23:17,065
Scott: But in this feudal Japan, there is one difference.

315
00:23:17,786 --> 00:23:27,180
Scott: And that difference is that there is a horrible disease that they can't really understand because their technology is limited and their understanding of disease is limited.

316
00:23:27,840 --> 00:23:30,799
Scott: But the disease only seems to affect men.

317
00:23:31,222 --> 00:23:32,890
Rym: Like Why the Last Man style.

318
00:23:32,930 --> 00:23:34,840
Rym: Like all the men are dying everywhere in Japan.

319
00:23:35,481 --> 00:23:35,541
Scott: Yes.

320
00:23:35,561 --> 00:23:36,744
Scott: It's basically Why the Last Man.

321
00:23:36,824 --> 00:23:38,448
Scott: But it's not literally the last man.

322
00:23:38,468 --> 00:23:39,792
Scott: It's just men.

323
00:23:39,872 --> 00:23:43,000
Scott: everywhere are dying and they're running out of men.

324
00:23:43,360 --> 00:23:44,462
Scott: And there are so few of them.

325
00:23:44,843 --> 00:23:46,306
Scott: And now it's mostly women.

326
00:23:46,727 --> 00:23:53,160
Scott: And the only dudes left are basically old men who for some reason haven't gotten the disease and became old.

327
00:23:54,380 --> 00:23:55,402
Scott: Babies who?

328
00:23:55,903 --> 00:24:01,313
Scott: you just had the baby and it's fine, assuming it survived.

329
00:24:01,554 --> 00:24:03,257
Scott: How long will it live?

330
00:24:03,818 --> 00:24:04,820
Scott: Will it get the disease?

331
00:24:05,760 --> 00:24:15,800
Scott: And then the very rare number of middle-aged younger dudes who are somehow still alive but could die at any time.

332
00:24:17,143 --> 00:24:19,914
Rym: And this is set during the Tokugawa Shogunate basically.

333
00:24:20,055 --> 00:24:21,320
Rym: So that era of Japan.

334
00:24:23,286 --> 00:24:27,500
Scott: And it's like, hey, what would be different and the same about those times?

335
00:24:29,440 --> 00:24:43,260
Scott: And then what the thing is, it does sort of give you the broader picture, but it's called Oku because it's focusing on a place called the Oku, which is basically a harem of dudes.

336
00:24:44,062 --> 00:24:45,189
Scott: Because they ran out of dudes.

337
00:24:45,631 --> 00:24:47,140
Scott: So the Shogun is a woman.

338
00:24:47,521 --> 00:24:56,540
Rym: So think about the type of period drama where there's a Shogun and then he has his harem in the inner court and all that kind of drama if the lady's intriguing.

339
00:24:56,960 --> 00:24:59,419
Rym: Just invert the sexes and that's the show.

340
00:25:00,140 --> 00:25:02,548
Scott: Yeah, they're waiting for the Shogun to have a male child.

341
00:25:02,588 --> 00:25:04,755
Scott: that lives and doesn't happen.

342
00:25:04,876 --> 00:25:06,080
Scott: so they can't find an heir.

343
00:25:07,021 --> 00:25:13,460
Scott: So the woman heir has to become the Shogun just like Queen of England or whatever.

344
00:25:13,520 --> 00:25:17,560
Rym: And then bit by bit, women take over all of Japanese society.

345
00:25:18,240 --> 00:25:22,880
Scott: Yeah, they show them taking over leadership of villages as well in some scenes.

346
00:25:23,440 --> 00:25:36,700
Rym: And the cool part, the fascinating part that's fun is that it is written and portrayed such that it could have actually happened because it's sort of set during the period when Japan was super isolationist.

347
00:25:37,181 --> 00:25:45,980
Rym: And it even frames that isolationist era as being Japan as a nation hiding the fact that all the men are dying from the outside world.

348
00:25:47,502 --> 00:25:51,340
Rym: Because the disease has not spread as far as they know beyond the borders of Japan.

349
00:25:51,820 --> 00:25:52,201
Scott: Yeah, they're like.

350
00:25:52,241 --> 00:25:57,440
Scott: we can never let foreigners know that we're running out of men because then they'll just conquer us.

351
00:25:58,120 --> 00:26:02,720
Scott: So we have to sort of pretend that we have lots of men all the time.

352
00:26:04,603 --> 00:26:06,250
Scott: Don't let them come into our cities.

353
00:26:06,711 --> 00:26:08,680
Scott: Only let them visit us in this one area.

354
00:26:10,081 --> 00:26:15,880
Rym: And the dignitaries that visit have to visit the Oku so that all the people they see are men to kind of trick them.

355
00:26:16,480 --> 00:26:17,943
Scott: The Oku is basically the woman.

356
00:26:17,963 --> 00:26:28,739
Scott: Shogun keeps a whole harem of dudes around to make babies with but also to present to foreigners and to protect from disease and etc.

357
00:26:29,982 --> 00:26:37,660
Scott: And so the story is mostly just focused on what's happening with the Shogun and what's happening in that Oku among those dudes.

358
00:26:37,880 --> 00:26:45,700
Rym: And it'll like the show mentions off and on like there'll be broad context of the political situation with the Shogunate and Edo and everything going on outside.

359
00:26:46,060 --> 00:26:54,800
Rym: But it's really just a zoomed in like hyper dramatic story of individual people navigating the court intrigue of the Oku.

360
00:26:55,060 --> 00:26:56,197
Rym: Like that's what it really is.

361
00:26:56,741 --> 00:26:57,965
Scott: The other part is just the setting.

362
00:26:58,005 --> 00:26:58,747
Scott: The story is court dealings.

363
00:27:02,740 --> 00:27:12,659
Rym: Like episode one, if you watch that and nothing else, you might think, "Ooh, this is about this female Shogun figuring out what's going on and why things are the way they

364
00:27:12,759 --> 00:27:12,839
Rym: are.".

365
00:27:13,180 --> 00:27:20,620
Rym: But no, the whole rest of the show is really just all these men are having the kind of court drama that women would have in a typical story like this.

366
00:27:21,141 --> 00:27:27,540
Rym: And the inversion of the gender roles is a big part of what makes this shine because they kind of fully maximize.

367
00:27:27,940 --> 00:27:28,860
Scott: That's sort of the whole point.

368
00:27:29,280 --> 00:27:30,799
Scott: Yeah, but they really lean into it.

369
00:27:30,880 --> 00:27:32,535
Scott: There's no point to the show if they don't have that.

370
00:27:34,661 --> 00:27:43,920
Rym: And I guess what I like about it is that the characters are actually moderately complex, but more importantly, what they do is moderately complex.

371
00:27:44,841 --> 00:27:50,880
Rym: It's kind of hard to predict where it's going because the characters do change a lot as it goes on.

372
00:27:51,421 --> 00:27:56,720
Rym: Like the young kid who's with the beautiful priest eventually murders that cat and then becomes a warrior.

373
00:27:57,381 --> 00:28:04,780
Rym: People change a lot and they sometimes make pragmatic decisions, which in stories like this people often don't make pragmatic decisions.

374
00:28:04,920 --> 00:28:06,038
Rym: And I like that aspect of it.

375
00:28:06,662 --> 00:28:12,120
Scott: I mean, you have different people with different personalities and different motivations and backstories.

376
00:28:13,421 --> 00:28:27,100
Scott: And then people are often doing really extreme things and you might really like or dislike them based on the horrible or not horrible things that they do to each other or even to themselves.

377
00:28:27,740 --> 00:28:34,420
Scott: But in the end, they eventually sort of reveal everyone's reasons for doing the things that they do.

378
00:28:34,600 --> 00:28:40,200
Scott: And it's like none of the people are truly straight up evil, right?

379
00:28:40,640 --> 00:28:42,958
Scott: I mean, some of them are evil because they're still doing evil shit.

380
00:28:43,580 --> 00:28:51,999
Rym: The closest to evil, I would say, of the major characters is what's-her-name, the woman who sets it all up and is sort of like the Vizier.

381
00:28:52,382 --> 00:28:53,160
Scott: Exactly, right.

382
00:28:53,320 --> 00:29:00,700
Rym: She is bad and she is trying to solve a very different problem from what literally everyone else is trying to do.

383
00:29:01,463 --> 00:29:05,660
Scott: Right, it's like the whole show, the whole first arc at least, right?

384
00:29:05,760 --> 00:29:12,880
Scott: This woman who's like the old granny of the Oku who's sort of secretly controlling everything like an evil Vizier or whatever.

385
00:29:13,040 --> 00:29:22,300
Rym: And she's obsessed with the blood of the Shogun and trying to bring the male heir back and keep society exactly under this military dictatorship.

386
00:29:23,442 --> 00:29:26,399
Rym: And she basically ruins the lives of everyone around her by doing this.

387
00:29:27,085 --> 00:29:28,920
Scott: Yep, and she keeps doing all this evil shit.

388
00:29:29,120 --> 00:29:35,140
Scott: But then at the end, or close to the end, I guess spoilers, it's revealed.

389
00:29:36,201 --> 00:29:43,480
Scott: She's basically like, "Look, the reason I'm doing all this evil shit to make sure that all this happens is because we got to avoid

390
00:29:43,500 --> 00:29:43,640
Scott: war.".

391
00:29:44,922 --> 00:29:46,193
Scott: She's like, "Look what happened

392
00:29:46,234 --> 00:29:46,597
Scott: before.".

393
00:29:47,261 --> 00:29:54,020
Scott: She's old, she lived through the era where all these different warlords are just battling each other like it's freaking a game of samurai swords.

394
00:29:54,561 --> 00:30:04,619
Scott: And she's like, "Since the disease came along and whatnot and we've been keeping the status quo and keeping a Shogun on the throne, we've been able to avoid

395
00:30:04,679 --> 00:30:04,859
Scott: that.".

396
00:30:05,300 --> 00:30:11,036
Scott: And she's like, "I don't care if I had to fuck up the lives of some monk or murder some kid or force people to

397
00:30:11,297 --> 00:30:11,537
Scott: fuck.".

398
00:30:12,560 --> 00:30:16,257
Scott: She's like, "That is nothing compared to avoiding a whole ass

399
00:30:16,297 --> 00:30:16,498
Scott: war.".

400
00:30:17,183 --> 00:30:17,930
Scott: And it's like, "Well,

401
00:30:18,252 --> 00:30:18,555
Scott: dammit.".

402
00:30:19,161 --> 00:30:20,834
Scott: Everyone's sort of like, "Well, I guess you're right,

403
00:30:20,895 --> 00:30:21,238
Scott: granny.".

404
00:30:21,640 --> 00:30:27,620
Rym: Yeah, with the caveat of that was the rationale of the Shogunates, which really, they were just military dictatorships.

405
00:30:28,660 --> 00:30:29,262
Scott: Obvious, yes.

406
00:30:29,563 --> 00:30:34,459
Scott: But within the context of the show, the people are saying, "I guess you're right,

407
00:30:34,499 --> 00:30:34,780
Scott: granny.".

408
00:30:34,820 --> 00:30:37,219
Scott: That doesn't mean that the real-life granny was right.

409
00:30:37,820 --> 00:30:38,182
Rym: Yeah.

410
00:30:38,904 --> 00:30:43,520
Rym: Well, she's also, she's like, cruel for no reason sometimes.

411
00:30:44,140 --> 00:30:48,620
Scott: She could've, it's like, if you really think about it, it's like, you could've gotten the job done without the cruelty.

412
00:30:48,880 --> 00:30:49,181
Rym: Oh, yeah.

413
00:30:49,742 --> 00:31:01,260
Rym: Especially considering all the characters around her are usually finding much more nuanced ways to solve their problems, even though their problems are way more deadly to them.

414
00:31:03,101 --> 00:31:08,500
Scott: I mean, you could try to say some interpretation, like, "Well, she's old school, she doesn't know a newer, better way to do

415
00:31:08,520 --> 00:31:08,560
Scott: it.".

416
00:31:08,600 --> 00:31:10,304
Rym: I think that actually is a big interpretation.

417
00:31:10,344 --> 00:31:17,220
Rym: Like, she represents the old male-dominated order, and basically everyone else is just trying to navigate the world they're presented with.

418
00:31:17,520 --> 00:31:31,180
Rym: And she's trying to cling to an old world instead of transparently adapting to the new world, which might still have involved a conspiracy with the outside world, but she didn't need to kill that many people and fuck up that many lives along the way.

419
00:31:32,040 --> 00:31:33,160
Scott: She did not need to do that.

420
00:31:35,781 --> 00:31:47,280
Scott: But yeah, there's a lot of romance going on, obviously, between the Shogun and different dudes, and, you know, romantic tensions between people and whatnot in their situations.

421
00:31:48,561 --> 00:31:56,260
Scott: Being able to be together, not being able to be together, having to follow formal procedures, you know, and whatnot.

422
00:31:58,701 --> 00:32:00,946
Scott: People in the Oku don't really have their freedom, right?

423
00:32:00,986 --> 00:32:02,689
Scott: It's like they're basically prisoner dudes.

424
00:32:03,451 --> 00:32:06,998
Scott: They can't leave against their will.

425
00:32:07,038 --> 00:32:08,160
Scott: They're not happy about that.

426
00:32:08,440 --> 00:32:13,420
Rym: But some of them end up being okay with it because of the way they change it in some cases.

427
00:32:14,020 --> 00:32:22,140
Rym: There's definitely an arc where, over time, it's obvious that the people in the Oku eventually begin wielding the real power of the state later, but not initially.

428
00:32:22,240 --> 00:32:24,199
Rym: Initially, they're basically just figureheads and puppets.

429
00:32:24,881 --> 00:32:35,780
Scott: And also, you know, this first arc is like, you know, the current Shogun is trying to figure out the history, and she's reading basically the story of the first Shogun with the first Oku.

430
00:32:36,100 --> 00:32:41,720
Scott: And then if you were to read the manga and get the whole story, it goes Shogun by Shogun, and those are the arcs, right?

431
00:32:41,760 --> 00:32:45,519
Scott: It's like, all right, time for the second Shogun, who is the child of the first one, right?

432
00:32:46,520 --> 00:32:47,827
Scott: First woman Shogun, right?

433
00:32:47,847 --> 00:32:50,420
Scott: It doesn't tell the stories about the dude Shoguns of the past, right?

434
00:32:50,861 --> 00:32:59,560
Scott: And each one, and it shows, you know, how the first arc here that you're going to see on Netflix is showing you how the Oku was formed from nothing, basically.

435
00:33:00,421 --> 00:33:07,860
Scott: And then as you go on to the later arcs, the Oku is like established and has, you know, its way of being.

436
00:33:08,421 --> 00:33:14,780
Scott: And that becomes the tradition and evolves into the Oku that the Shogun of the first episode inherited.

437
00:33:15,683 --> 00:33:21,360
Rym: Well, like, you see, here is a tradition, and it's weird, and like, that's just the way things are done.

438
00:33:21,460 --> 00:33:26,560
Rym: And then in the flashbacks, you see, like, literally the exact incident that led to that tradition.

439
00:33:28,102 --> 00:33:31,760
Rym: The beheading of the first person to have sex with the Shogun, like, that whole thing is one of the examples.

440
00:33:32,464 --> 00:33:33,278
Scott: That's the biggest one.

441
00:33:34,443 --> 00:33:41,920
Scott: But it has the typical- There's plenty of things where it shows you something of the cart, the present, and it's like, how did things get that way?

442
00:33:42,241 --> 00:33:45,218
Scott: And then it's like, here's how things got that way, oh.

443
00:33:46,060 --> 00:33:49,700
Rym: But I think the show does a good job of showing rather than telling.

444
00:33:50,641 --> 00:34:04,940
Rym: Because for a show based on a manga that is mostly just beautiful people standing there talking to each other, the anime actually is animated pretty well and keeps things moving, and it isn't just people standing there talking to each other the whole time.

445
00:34:06,421 --> 00:34:13,620
Scott: I don't know about the difficulty of animating it, considering, you know, so many computers and not a lot of action scenes.

446
00:34:14,199 --> 00:34:15,485
Scott: It's like, things don't move a lot.

447
00:34:16,370 --> 00:34:18,400
Scott: It's people sitting, people standing.

448
00:34:20,342 --> 00:34:23,820
Rym: I'd say the show is carried by the quality of the voice acting more than anything.

449
00:34:24,260 --> 00:34:33,060
Scott: Yeah, the voice acting, but also, even though there's not a lot of movement that's happening, it's a lot of illustration, right?

450
00:34:33,080 --> 00:34:37,899
Scott: Because they have to illustrate buildings, scenes, clothing.

451
00:34:39,280 --> 00:34:46,637
Rym: The setting of the oku is so well defined, and there's a lot of subtle detail that the characters interact with.

452
00:34:46,838 --> 00:34:50,679
Rym: that's never called out, and I think that is the strongest aspect of this show for me.

453
00:34:51,400 --> 00:34:57,820
Rym: It's got all the period things of that era of Japan just there, and as far as I know, relatively accurate.

454
00:34:58,381 --> 00:35:05,557
Rym: And like the furniture, and the screens, and all that kind of stuff, and the characters interact with the stuff all the time, and they're plot points, but they're never called out.

455
00:35:05,598 --> 00:35:06,179
Rym: They're just there.

456
00:35:08,962 --> 00:35:11,632
Rym: So yeah, I'd say it's worth watching.

457
00:35:11,653 --> 00:35:13,600
Rym: It's on Netflix if you've got Netflix.

458
00:35:13,800 --> 00:35:22,260
Scott: The people who like, you know, a gender, I guess it's not, you know, it's not too gender bendy, or twisty, or changey.

459
00:35:22,320 --> 00:35:25,933
Scott: At least not, you know, there haven't been trans people or gay people.

460
00:35:27,680 --> 00:35:31,939
Rym: There's a lot of LGBT representation that one couldn't have hoped from something like this.

461
00:35:33,362 --> 00:35:36,780
Scott: There might be in a later part, but there isn't in the parts that I've seen.

462
00:35:37,000 --> 00:35:40,620
Rym: Yeah, it mostly only focuses on the flipped binary.

463
00:35:41,360 --> 00:35:41,781
Scott: Yeah.

464
00:35:44,085 --> 00:35:51,659
Scott: It's like, you know, it takes some of the, you know, typically male traits and flips them over backwards and forwards, just like Why the Last Man does.

465
00:35:52,680 --> 00:35:54,559
Scott: I don't know which came first.

466
00:35:56,502 --> 00:36:01,020
Rym: But like, you get the, like the men in the harem in the later, like the first episode.

467
00:36:01,401 --> 00:36:06,740
Rym: They're all like fighting with each other over petty nonsense and doing all the stuff that you expect from harem dramas.

468
00:36:07,200 --> 00:36:19,480
Rym: And it's just, it's dudes doing it, and they're kind of doing it exactly, like, it very clearly has this undercurrent of, if you flip the gender roles of a society just one for one, they're the same either way.

469
00:36:19,601 --> 00:36:21,954
Rym: Like, there's nothing to do with the underlying people.

470
00:36:21,975 --> 00:36:22,900
Rym: It's just a circumstance.

471
00:36:27,002 --> 00:36:29,370
Rym: This has been GeekNights with Rym and Scott.

472
00:36:29,431 --> 00:36:31,940
Rym: Special thanks to DJ Pretzel for the opening theme.

473
